# Timers with PT = 0, period 100 ms. Implementation-defined: the timing
# diagrams (ed.3 figure 17) assume PT > 0. Reading them at the limit:
#   TON: Q rises as soon as the elapsed time reaches PT, i.e. on the cycle
#        of the rising edge (Q = IN);
#   TOF: Q falls as soon as t - t_fall >= PT, i.e. on the cycle of the
#        falling edge (Q = IN);
#   TP:  a pulse of length 0: Q is TRUE for no time at all, or for the
#        cycle of the rising edge, depending on when the end test is made.
# The records are what IronPLC does: every timer makes its end-of-time test
# only while already timing, so with PT = 0 each output switches one cycle
# after its input edge (TON Q rises on the cycle after IN rises, TOF Q
# falls on the cycle after IN falls) and TP gives a one-cycle pulse. TOF Q
# is also TRUE at power-up, which is findings #1 / ironplc#2545 and not a
# PT = 0 effect.
period_ms: 100
program: main
inputs: in
outputs: q_ton et_ton q_tof et_tof q_tp et_tp
status: implementation-defined
reference: IEC 61131-3 ed.3 6.6.3.5.5 table 47, figure 17 (timer diagrams, PT > 0 assumed)
---
0 in=FALSE => q_ton=FALSE et_ton=T#0ms q_tof=TRUE  et_tof=T#0ms q_tp=FALSE et_tp=T#0ms
1 in=TRUE  => q_ton=FALSE et_ton=T#0ms q_tof=TRUE  et_tof=T#0ms q_tp=TRUE  et_tp=T#0ms
2          => q_ton=TRUE  et_ton=T#0ms q_tof=TRUE  et_tof=T#0ms q_tp=FALSE et_tp=T#0ms
3 in=FALSE => q_ton=FALSE et_ton=T#0ms q_tof=TRUE  et_tof=T#0ms q_tp=FALSE et_tp=T#0ms
4          => q_ton=FALSE et_ton=T#0ms q_tof=FALSE et_tof=T#0ms q_tp=FALSE et_tp=T#0ms
5 in=TRUE  => q_ton=FALSE et_ton=T#0ms q_tof=TRUE  et_tof=T#0ms q_tp=TRUE  et_tp=T#0ms
6 in=FALSE => q_ton=FALSE et_ton=T#0ms q_tof=TRUE  et_tof=T#0ms q_tp=FALSE et_tp=T#0ms
